changed@sim.uucp writes:

[in response to: fixed armor class => invincibility. no fun! --dave--]

>I have a few problems with this:

[he writes about the wizard of yendor's extreme experience enabling the
  wizard to hit against normal armor classes and how this is unfair with
  the random AC factor thrown in. --dave--]

in previous versions of hack i always tried to sneak the amulet away
from the wizard and then escape without too much difficulty. i was
unable to do that in nh3 in part because the castle's been rebuilt and
in part because the wizard appears to be much more clever. i got the
amulet and managed to get back up to level one where i was poised to
win the game ...

>Tell me that it's too easy and no fun AFTER you've actually won.
>Most people can't even _get_ to the Wizard, and going back up is a lot
>tougher than the trip down.

i agree, most people will not get to the wizard easily if they continue
to play in a "hack and slash" manner. and going back up was indeed
tougher than going down. instead of getting easier the game seemed to
get harder and harder the better i got. that was fun!!! but i didn't
win this game either ... it seems that there is a new feature in nh3
(that i found delightful) that prevents you from simply walking out of
the dungeon untested. eventually the wizard showed up again and stole
the amulet back! i fell asleep! and awoke with a Vampire lord breathing
down my neck. seemed ironic since i had just been one myself!!!

>It doesn't go both ways -- you don't get to strike at a modified
>negative armor class against monsters the way they can at you.

Xorns can't teleport at will, Rust Monsters can't self-polymorph,
Balrogs can't see invisible, Red nagas won't violate the written word.
you can [and should] do all of these and more! besides the gods are on
your side (or should be if you're doing things right). would you have the
monsters acquire these abilities so that you could have fixed AC???

>So has anyone actually won the real version of NH3.0?  If so, what
>were your AC and primary weapon?

i didn't win, has anyone? under previous rules i would have won with:
AC-16, blessed +6 Snickersnee, Hp193, all stats > 13 and St18/87 in
particular, Exp19 (damn Wraiths don't play fair and leave corpses when
i kill them) and various intrinsic attributes. also my armor provided
some additional magic (i.e. St25, speed, magic protection, reflection).
what i noticed was that the game has various stages, early on the
monsters simply overrun you if you're not lucky (and clever). after
awhile you can hold your own if you've got a magic edge (i.e. invisiblity
or real 2x speed). things get easier when you possess and use the rings
of power (there were 6 that i found essential). knowing certain
powerful spells was a big help too. and then there's the wizard ... i
rarely tried to fight him toe-to-toe. when i confronted the wizard i
tried not to be myself, sort of. i thoroughly enjoyed that game even
though i didn't win. i learned alot but i've got a feeling that there
is still alot to learn about the game. in nh2.3 i had won under every
character, more than once, with scores better than 2M, and had two
streaks of 4 consecutive wins. most of the other players in that
dungeon had similar stats. we were pretty bored with nh2.3 and resorted
to devising scoring/ranking schemes to determine who was actually the
best player. in nh3, so far, the best is the one with the best score.
it seems to take numerous false starts before you get a game that's
playable ...

>Ed

and i would like to thank you for your attention. nh3 is real hard now,
but i suspect the difficulty level will gradually decrease for the masses
as information is gained and shared ... [sometime around 1999  8^] ]

